      <!-- Section 4: Additive Selection: Permitted vs Prohibited Chemistries -->
      <h2>Additive Selection: Permitted vs Prohibited Chemistries</h2>

      <p>
        The additive package available to food-grade lubricant formulators is dramatically more constrained than that available for conventional industrial lubricants. Every additive must appear on the NSF White List, and each ingredient's maximum permitted concentration in the finished product is prescribed. This regulatory ceiling forces formulators to achieve performance targets with a much smaller additive toolkit than their conventional counterparts.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Permitted antioxidants</strong> include food-grade phenolic antioxidants such as BHA (butylated hydroxyanisole, 21 CFR 172.110), BHT (butylated hydroxytoluene, 21 CFR 172.115), and tocopherol blends. These provide adequate oxidative stability for WMO-based H1 lubricants at moderate operating temperatures. For high-temperature PAO or ester applications, phosphite co-antioxidants on the White List provide enhanced hydroperoxide decomposition at elevated temperatures.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Corrosion inhibitors</strong> approved for H1 use include certain organic fatty acid derivatives, modified imidazolines, and a limited set of carboxylate-based rust inhibitors. The potent ashless dithiophosphate and sulphonate rust inhibitor packages used routinely in conventional lubricants are largely excluded from H1 formulations due to their non-listed status.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Antiwear (AW) and extreme pressure (EP) additives</strong> represent the most significant formulation challenge in food-grade lubricants. The high-performance zinc dialkyldithiophosphate (ZDDP) additive system that provides AW/EP, antioxidant, and anticorrosion multifunctionality in conventional lubricants is not approved for H1 use. Approved food-grade AW chemistries include certain phosphate esters (e.g., tricresyl phosphate is excluded — toxic, but some alkyl phosphates are White Listed), polyol ester base fluids themselves (which provide intrinsic lubricity superior to WMO), and food-grade wax esters. As a result, food-grade gear oils and EP greases necessarily carry a heavier load on base fluid selection to compensate for restricted additive EP performance.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Thickeners for food-grade greases</strong> must also meet H1 requirements. Aluminium complex soap thickeners with food-grade fatty acid and aluminium sources are widely used in H1 greases and provide good high-temperature resistance and water-washing resistance. Polyurea thickeners (aliphatic diisocyanate-based) and PTFE (polytetrafluoroethylene) dry-film thickeners are also available with H1 registration for specialist applications. Lithium 12-hydroxystearate, the workhorse thickener of conventional greases, is not on the White List for H1 use — a fact that surprises many formulators encountering food-grade grease development for the first time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Our <a href="/lubricant-formulations-and-technology" target="_top">lubricant formulation technology</a> service covers detailed additive package development and White List compliance screening for food-grade lubricant projects.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 5: Comparison Table: NSF H1 vs Conventional Industrial Lubricants -->
      <h2>NSF H1 vs Conventional Industrial Lubricants</h2>

      <p>
